WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:06.848
Quadrature phase-shift keying, or QPSK,
carries two bits in each symbol.

2
00:00:06.848 --> 00:00:14.018
We group the incoming stream into pairs and
map each pair to one of four points in the

3
00:00:14.018 --> 00:00:15.611
in-phase and quadrature plane.

4
00:00:15.611 --> 00:00:20.598
Neighboring points are often assigned bit
patterns that differ by one bit, a Gray

5
00:00:20.598 --> 00:00:26.297
mapping that reduces the number of bit errors
when noise pushes a symbol across one nearby

6
00:00:26.297 --> 00:00:26.653
boundary.

7
00:00:26.653 --> 00:00:32.626
The constellation is more than a diagram: it
is the transmitter's vocabulary and the

8
00:00:32.626 --> 00:00:33.906
receiver's decision map.

9
00:00:34.010 --> 00:00:39.249
Each constellation point supplies two
coordinates: I for the cosine carrier and Q

10
00:00:39.249 --> 00:00:40.861
for the sine carrier.

11
00:00:40.861 --> 00:00:46.486
The carriers are ninety degrees apart, so they
are orthogonal over the symbol interval.

12
00:00:46.486 --> 00:00:52.514
We weight them by the chosen coordinates and
add them to create one real passband waveform.

13
00:00:52.514 --> 00:00:58.321
Changing the bit pair changes the carrier
phase while maintaining a similar envelope.

14
00:00:58.321 --> 00:01:04.332
Orthogonality lets the receiver later separate
the two components, provided timing and

15
00:01:04.332 --> 00:01:06.837
carrier reference are sufficiently aligned.

16
00:01:06.962 --> 00:01:09.359
A real channel changes what arrives.

17
00:01:09.359 --> 00:01:13.839
Thermal noise spreads each ideal point into a
cloud.

18
00:01:13.839 --> 00:01:18.500
Frequency or phase offset rotates the
constellation.

19
00:01:18.500 --> 00:01:24.581
Fading changes amplitude, and multipath can
smear one symbol into the next.

20
00:01:24.581 --> 00:01:28.214
These effects are different and require
different corrections.

21
00:01:28.214 --> 00:01:34.900
A receiver chain may estimate timing, carrier
phase, channel gain, and equalization before

22
00:01:34.900 --> 00:01:36.443
making bit decisions.

23
00:01:36.443 --> 00:01:43.390
Looking only at a waveform can hide the
pattern; the constellation exposes whether

24
00:01:43.390 --> 00:01:48.200
uncertainty is random spread, systematic
rotation, scaling, or intersymbol

25
00:01:48.200 --> 00:01:48.734
interference.

26
00:01:48.842 --> 00:01:54.532
After synchronization and matched filtering,
the receiver samples once per symbol and

27
00:01:54.532 --> 00:01:56.903
selects the closest valid point.

28
00:01:56.903 --> 00:02:03.075
In basic QPSK, the I and Q signs define four
decision regions.

29
00:02:03.075 --> 00:02:09.065
A sample near a boundary is fragile because a
small noise change can flip the decision.

30
00:02:09.065 --> 00:02:14.952
Increasing signal energy separates the
clusters relative to noise, reducing error

31
00:02:14.952 --> 00:02:19.234
probability, but power and bandwidth are
limited resources.

32
00:02:19.234 --> 00:02:25.895
Receiver design therefore balances reliable
decisions against spectral efficiency, energy,

33
00:02:25.895 --> 00:02:27.893
and implementation complexity.

34
00:02:28.010 --> 00:02:35.035
The full reasoning chain is compact: group
bits, map a symbol, modulate orthogonal

35
00:02:35.035 --> 00:02:40.440
carriers, pass through the channel,
synchronize, filter, sample, decide, and

36
00:02:40.440 --> 00:02:40.980
decode.

37
00:02:40.980 --> 00:02:45.017
To debug a link, compare evidence at each
boundary.

38
00:02:45.017 --> 00:02:51.395
Verify the bit mapping, inspect carrier
frequency and timing, plot the received

39
00:02:51.395 --> 00:02:56.710
constellation, measure error-vector magnitude,
and compare transmitted with recovered bits.

40
00:02:56.710 --> 00:03:02.416
This stepwise view turns an abstract
modulation formula into a practical

41
00:03:02.416 --> 00:03:03.454
engineering workflow.
